# GitHub CI often executes from `main`

Many GitHub Actions setups load workflow definitions from `main` (or another
tracked default branch). Edits on a feature branch or only in the working
tree have **no effect** on that path until the tracked branch sees them —
unless the workflow is explicitly triggered from the PR head (most
`pull_request` jobs use the merge/head ref for *running* the job, but
**workflow file changes** for some triggers still only apply after merge).

## Rule

When you change `.github/workflows/**`:

1. Follow `github-actions` + `code-yaml` for the YAML itself.
2. Commit related files together (workflow plus scripts/actions it calls)
   when the user asks to commit.
3. Remind the user that runners pick up definition changes according to the
   trigger — default-branch-only paths need the commit on that branch.
4. Do **not** commit or push automatically unless the consuming project has
   an explicit preference for that.

## Why

SCM-driven CI checks out a specific ref. A workflow change is “live” for
default-branch triggers only once that branch has the commit. Do not claim a
workflow edit was verified in CI until a run that could see the new file has
finished (`github-pr-checks`).
